    <div class="crs-pin-detail is-open" data-detail="m2-know-0"><strong>Site Development Plan</strong><p>Knowing where each phase or building actually sits lets you answer "which block faces the main road" or "how far is my unit from the entrance" without fumbling for a map in front of the client.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m2-know-1"><strong>Amenity Locations</strong><p>The pool, clubhouse, retail row, and parks are often what sells the lifestyle before the unit itself does — know exactly where they are and what's already operational.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m2-know-2"><strong>Turnover Status per Phase</strong><p>Some phases may already be turned over and lived-in while others are still under construction. Mixing these up in front of a client instantly damages your credibility.</p></div>

    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>Instead of opening with "This unit is 24 square meters with a balcony," you open with "This community has a resort-style pool, a co-working lounge, and 24/7 security, five minutes from the nearest mall and business district." Once the client is sold on the lifestyle, the specific unit details land far better.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> When presenting a development, always sell the macro (the community lifestyle and amenities) before selling the micro (the specific unit).</div>
</article>

<article class="crs-lesson">
    <h3><span class="crs-lesson-num">Lesson 2.2</span> Explaining Value Drivers</h3>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">2.2.1 The House Isn't What Moves the Price</span>
    <p>Property values in the Philippines rarely move because of the house alone — they move because of what's happening around it. Agents who track local infrastructure and zoning changes can explain, and sometimes even anticipate, why a property is likely to appreciate long before the general public catches on.</p>
    <p>This is the difference between reciting a listing and actually advising a client. Anyone can read a spec sheet aloud; being able to say "here's why this area specifically is worth watching over the next three to five years" is what makes a client trust your judgment on price.</p>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">2.2.2 What Actually Drives Appreciation</span>
    <p>These are the signals worth watching in any area you sell in:</p>
    <ul class="crs-list">
        <li><strong>Transit access</strong> — a new expressway exit, an MRT/LRT extension, or the North-South Commuter Railway can cut commute times dramatically once completed.</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial growth</strong> — a new mall, office park, or business district nearby brings jobs, foot traffic, and demand for housing.</li>
        <li><strong>Institutions</strong> — a well-regarded school or hospital moving into the area tends to pull families and long-term buyers toward it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>None of these signals move a price overnight. What they do is tell you — and let you tell your client — which direction an area is heading, so a "why here, why now" question has a real, specific answer instead of a generic "it's a good investment."</p>
    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>A property near a planned expressway interchange in Laguna may look "in the middle of nowhere" today. But if you can explain the interchange's expected completion date and its impact on commute time to Makati or BGC, you help the client see the investment angle — not just the current view from the window.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> Value is rarely just about the house itself. It is driven heavily by proximity to economic hubs and future city planning. You must know your local municipality's future development plans.</div>
</article>

<article class="crs-lesson">
    <h3><span class="crs-lesson-num">Lesson 2.3</span> Matching Properties to Client Goals</h3>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">2.3.1 One Buyer Profile Doesn't Fit All</span>
    <p>Not every buyer needs the same type of property, and sending the same shortlist to everyone is a fast way to waste both your time and theirs. Tap each buyer profile below to see what usually matters most to them.</p>
    <div class="crs-compare-grid">
        <div class="crs-compare-card">
            <div class="crs-compare-head"><strong>🏡 The Growing Family</strong><svg class="crs-compare-toggle" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"/></svg></div>
            <div class="crs-compare-body"><p>Usually prioritizes space, a nearby school, and a subdivision with a playground — a house-and-lot often fits better than a compact condo.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="crs-compare-card">
            <div class="crs-compare-head"><strong>💼 The Young Professional</strong><svg class="crs-compare-toggle" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 4v16m8-8H4"/></svg></div>
            <div class="crs-compare-body"><p>Usually prioritizes commute time, security, and a lock-and-leave lifestyle — a transit-oriented condo close to a CBD is often a much stronger fit than a house two hours away.</p></div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>These two profiles are just a starting point — real clients often blend traits from both, or bring priorities neither profile covers. The goal isn't to force every buyer into a category, it's to notice which needs are actually driving their decision so your shortlist reflects them.</p>
    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>A young professional working in Ortigas tells you their main concern is commute time. Instead of sending listings across five different cities, you shortlist three condo options within a 15-minute radius of their office — respecting their time and dramatically increasing the chance of a decision within one or two site visits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> Showing fewer, highly targeted properties is much more effective than showing 15 random homes. Quality of matching always beats quantity of showings.</div>
</article>

<div class="crs-callout">
    <strong>📌 TAKE NOTE — Know Your Ground</strong>
    <p>You don't need to be a licensed urban planner, but you should always be able to answer: "Why will this area be worth more in five years?" If you can't answer that, spend time with your municipality's zoning maps and infrastructure roadmap before your next site visit.</p>
</div>

<div class="crs-key-takeaways">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Sell the community and lifestyle first, then the specific unit.</li>
        <li>Property value is driven by infrastructure, zoning, and future development — not just the structure itself.</li>
        <li>Stay informed on your local municipality's infrastructure and zoning plans.</li>
        <li>Match a small number of highly relevant properties to the client's actual goals instead of showing everything available.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
